      "abstract": "Contamination caused by potentially toxic metals, such as lead (Pb) and zinc (Zn), poses a threat to human health and ecological balance. It is crucial to explore options for remediating contaminated areas. The immobilization of metals in the soil matrix depends on several factors, and the application of organic materials with high adsorption capacity, such as biochar, can favor these processes. Additionally, converting waste into biochar contributes to environmental sustainability. This study aimed to evaluate the adsorption capacity of Pb and Zn by a soil from Ribeira Valley (southeastern Brazil) before and after the addition of biochar derived from urban pruning waste, in proportions of 1 and 5% (by mass). Batch equilibrium tests were performed varying the soil/solution ratio (1/5, 1/10, 1/20, 1/50, and 1/100) and the initial concentration of Pb and Zn (195 and 390 mg L^−1). The addition of biochar to the soil increased the percentage of adsorption of both metals. In the test with an initial concentration of 195 mg L^−1 and a soil/solution ratio of 1/10, the Pb removal efficiency of the unamended soil was 55.8%. This efficiency increased to 99.4% when 5% biochar was added to the soil. Regarding Zn adsorption, the efficiency increased from 18.3% to 68.0% under the same conditions. However, for some soil-solution ratios, there was no statistical difference between the adsorption of the soil and the mixture with 1% biochar, suggesting that this amount of biochar may not have been sufficient to generate a significant effect. The addition of biochar can be considered an alternative for soil improvement in contaminated areas and provides a beneficial use for urban pruning waste.",
