      "abstract": "This research underscores the importance of integrating hydrogeophysics with numerical modeling to evaluate soil infiltration capacity at two surface infiltration system locations in the Mornag and Korba plains of Northeast Tunisia, specifically designed for artificial groundwater recharge. Numerical modeling serves a dual purpose, facilitating both the design and efficient management of the infiltration basins. The methodology entails the application of time-lapse electrical resistivity tomography (ERT) imaging during two controlled infiltration tests. Subsequently, the ERT data is utilized to calibrate a variably saturated flow model (Hydrus2D/3D). In-depth soil investigations were conducted through the implementation of boreholes with a depth of 20 meters and two ERT Wenner array configuration sections positioned perpendicular to each other, featuring low resolution with 5 m electrode spacing. The estimated saturated conductivity (Ks) was found to be approximately 2.86 × 10^–5 m/s and 2.4 × 10^–5 m/s for the Mornag and Korba sites, respectively. These values align with sandy soil characteristics and are corroborated by laboratory measurements. Water flow simulations, utilizing Hydrus2D/3D, incorporated the calibration of Van Genuchten’s parameters based on water content derived from the ERT dataset. The resulting calibrated model is intended for application in the design and management of forthcoming infiltration basins.",
