      "abstract": "Plastic waste is often diverted to landfills where it is exposed to the natural elements, gets weathered and disintegrates into micro-sized particles, generalized as microplastics (MPs), into the landfill leachate. The weathering process, driven by solar radiation, alters the plastic properties and affects the disintegration process. Therefore, it is important to understand the leaching behavior of MPs from plastic waste. In this study, the leaching behavior of weathered and non-weathered polyethylene terephthalate (PET) bottles and polyvinyl chloride (PVC) film was used to assess the effect of weathering on the leachability of the materials. The samples were exposed to artificial UV light for 5 days to simulate weathering. MPs were leached from weathered and non-weathered plastic samples using synthetic leachate at a ratio of 1:100 plastic-to-liquid. The results show that weathering significantly affects the leachability of PVC film but did not affect the leachability of PET plastic. Weathered PVC film leached the most MPs (approximately 10,987–16,480 particles/L), followed by non-weathered PVC film (4760–6947 particles/L). Weathered and non-weathered PET plastic, on the other hand, leached approximately 267–427 particles/L. The FTIR results comparing normal and weathered PET plastic samples show no differences in the spectra. On the other hand, the FTIR results for the PVC samples show a decrease in the absorbance values at the peaks near 1250 and 610 cm^−1, with the weathered samples showing smaller absorbance values. The results of this study indicate that exposure to UV radiation during use and in landfills can noticeably impact the leachability of MPs from PVC.",
