      "abstract": "Coastal erosion is one of the most critical concerns worldwide, with 70% of the world’s sandy beaches undergoing erosion. One of the factors contributing to that phenomenon is the multiple and continuous exchanges of sediment particles along the beach profile, as a consequence of the decrease in granulometry due to particle attrition. This work aims to determine the relationship between the areas of highest vulnerability to weathering and median sediment size on the Mediterranean coast of the Comunidad Valenciana (Spain). For this purpose, 850 sand samples were collected from 177 beaches along the Valencian coast. The samples were transported to the laboratory where they were washed to remove the sea salt particles, dried in an oven and granulometrically measured to obtain, among other parameters, the median sediment size. In addition, for at least one sample from each of the beaches, the accelerated particle wear test (APW) was performed. Subsequently, the sediment quality classification index (SQCI) was obtained. The results show that 66% of the samples are classified as moderate quality, 15% as good quality, 8% as poor quality, 3% as very poor quality and 7% as very good quality. It is also observed that the worst samples are those with the lowest number of cycles in APW and the highest weight loss in the first cycle. However, the median sediment size has no direct relationship with sediment quality. Finally, the samples with the highest vulnerability to erosion are located in areas close to the mouth of ravines, whereas the best quality samples are located in natural areas, or in other words, the ones with less anthropic impact.",
